# AGENTS.md

Outline: one line per paragraph or list the final wording will hold. `[home]` marks a rule
whose one home is this file; everything else here points elsewhere.

## How to start

- [home] Every session reads this file, then the role file its start instruction names:
  `docs/agents/planner.md`, `docs/agents/builder.md` or `docs/agents/reviewer.md`.
- [home] The start instruction names the role, the target and the skills, the same text in
  every harness, e.g. `Role: Builder. Ticket: #12. Skills: tdd.`
- [home] A session holds one role. A subagent acts in its parent's role, named in its task.
- [home] A session started without a role is read-only. habit's explicit request for a
  change makes it a Builder; `builder.md` says how a direct request becomes a ticket.

## Flow at a glance

- [home] Five lines: an idea or issue reaches a Planner; habit approves the ticket; habit
  starts a Builder; the PR declares its risk tier and gets that tier's reviews; habit
  reads the merge summary and merges with the pinned command.
- Detail lives in the role files and `docs/agents/review.md`; this section only orients.

## Hard limits

- [home] No agent merges, enables auto-merge, force-pushes, rewrites pushed history, or
  changes rulesets, repository settings, Actions settings or Actions secrets.
- [home] No agent calls habit's account alias, even when a handoff shows habit a command
  that uses it.
- [home] No agent discards or rewrites work it doesn't own: other sessions' branches,
  worktrees or uncommitted changes.
- [home] Planner: writes the tracker; writes the repository only for `GLOSSARY.md` and
  ADRs, through its own PR; never writes product code.
- [home] Builder: one ticket, one branch, one PR; changes its ticket only after habit's
  recorded agreement.
- [home] Reviewer: never pushes, never edits the PR, tickets or labels.

## Talking to habit and to other agents

- [home] Agent to agent: GitHub, in English. Nothing a later session needs lives only in a
  terminal.
- [home] Agent to habit: the terminal, in plain Chinese, with no code, diffs or paths.
  Each message names its sender and target, e.g. "Builder · PR #12".
- [home] Asking habit to start a session: two marked parts, "给 habit" (where to open it,
  which model, why) and "粘贴给 agent" (the start instruction). Never a list of options.
- [home] An escalation puts the full question in the terminal and one "Waiting on habit: …"
  line on the PR or ticket.
- [home] habit's terminal decisions that a later session needs are written back to GitHub.

## Governance files

- [home] Files that open with the generated notice are fixed: only the generator changes
  them. Need a change? Tell habit; it is a governance model change, not a PR here.
- [home] The governance record, `.github/governance/record.yml`, holds the confirmed values:
  repository-specific sensitive paths, high floors, required CI checks. An agent changes
  them only in a delivery that quotes habit's request verbatim, never on its own, even to
  add protection; it proposes instead.
- [home] Where a skill's text disagrees with this repository's committed governance, the
  governance wins; report the conflict to habit.
- [home] A skill a role file lists that is missing from the machine stops the session; tell
  habit.

## Code Review Rules

Every reviewer reads this section: the Codex review bot natively, Bugbot through
`.cursor/BUGBOT.md`, the Reviewer and the self-verification subagent through their files.

- [home] Risk tiers, one table: low / medium / high, each with its criteria and examples.
  A change takes the highest tier any part reaches. Trusted authors: habit and the agent
  account.
- [home] Sensitive paths are listed once, in `.github/governance/record.yml`. A change to one
  is at least its floor (medium unless the record says high).
- [home] Finding categories: Important (blocks while open), Nit, Pre-existing; one line each.
- [home] Mapping: Codex P0/P1 → Important; Bugbot blocking → Important, non-blocking → Nit;
  security HIGH/MEDIUM → Important, LOW → Nit; "not introduced by this change" →
  Pre-existing.
- [home] Tier challenge: the PR body's `Risk:` line below what the change warrants is an
  Important finding, naming the warranted tier.
- [home] An ordinary PR that edits fixed governance content is an Important finding.
- [home] From round 2: review only what changed plus open findings; never repeat a finding;
  name each finding's bug class; Nits in round 1 only, at most three.

## Where to find more

| Task | Read |
| --- | --- |
| Your role's workflow | `docs/agents/<role>.md` |
| Tiers in practice, review minimums, findings, rounds, merge summary, security review | `docs/agents/review.md` |
| Issues, specs, tickets, wayfinder maps | `docs/agents/issue-tracker.md` |
| Triage labels | `docs/agents/triage-labels.md` |
| Domain terms and decisions | `docs/agents/domain.md` |
| Build, lint, test, CI | `docs/agents/build.md` |

